James wrote:

> In the end, if you're writing C, you define a struct, and a lot
> of functions to manipulate it, and cross your fingers that no
> one touches it otherwise.  

Well, no.  If I'm working in C and want a struct type to be opaque 
(ie, code in other files can't directly mess with it)  I let other 
code use pointers to the struct type, but not the type itself.  To
express this using C's standard scoping rules, I put 

/* actortype - the type of anything in the game that can be used 
   and specialized with triggers or methods in one of the principal 
   roles in an actiontype - includes all monsters, most items, 
   traps, etc. */
#typedef struct actorstruct * actortype

in actor.h, where everybody can see it, along with the function 
prototypes, and then 

struct actorstruct {
   loctype location;
   spectype species; /* or kind, for inanimate actors*/
   attrlist attributes;
   int maxhealth; /* also used as structural integrity for 
                     inanimate actors */
   int currentdamage;
   int size;  /* exponential scale, human == 0, mass doubles 
                 every 10 points of size, linear dimension 
                 every 30. rings(-180) are the smallest actors. */   
   float speed; 
   /* more stuff here if the game requires it and it can't or 
      shouldn't be managed as an attribute */
};

in actor.c along with the functions.  Everybody can see actortype 
and use it, but only the functions in the same file with struct 
actorstruct can see into its structure and manipulate its fields. 

Everybody else has to call those functions to do anything; otherwise
they are looking at a raw pointer value and have no information about
the structure at the other end of it.  If they try to reference 
its fields by name, they get an error.  

This is a bog-standard application of scope rules in C to achieve 
opaque datatypes.  Everyone who has ever done large projects in C 
knows how to do this, it's a standard idiom. 

And if you're still annoyed because some deliberately bad code can 
cast pointers to a different type and manipulate things that way, 
I can tell you three things.  First, you can still do that in C++.  
Second, it's very easy for a source checkin script to spot explicit 
pointer casts and refuse to accept code containing them. Third, you
shouldn't tolerate deliberately bad code in the project.

C++ added some syntax so people could avoid the need to understand 
C's scoping rules and use them, and some bondage-and-discipline to 
thwart a few (very few) of the sorts of deliberately bad code that 
should never have been tolerated anyway.  It's worthwhile for a 
lot of programmers, but hardly magical or revolutionary.

